WEBVTT

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:16.120
- Hi, welcome back.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:17.980
Certainly glad you could join me today,

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.580
because today I have a special treat for you.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.480
Every so often, we have a young man on the show

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.870
that he's just done some fantastic paintings,

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:27.560
and we just get hundreds of letters about this young man.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.080
In fact, I had a letter from a lady the other day.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:30.000
She says,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.340
you give him his own series,

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:34.010
because he explains things better than you do.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:35.670
Well, he's after my job.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:39.730
So today, it's my pleasure and I'm just proud

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:43.150
as any father could be to introduce my son, Steve, to you.

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:44.910
And I'm gonna turn Steve loose here.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.090
He's gonna show you how to do a fantastic painting

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:48.780
in just a couple of minutes.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.820
So I tell you what, I'm gonna turn it over to Steve,

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.460
and I'll see you later, Steve.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.230
- Thanks, Dan.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:56.910
Today we're gonna do something a little bit different,

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.740
and they're gonna be running the colors

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.000
across the bottom of the screen

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:05.980
you. I'm going to leave the top sort of like open vignette effect this time and let the

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:13.750
sky just mist out into the painting. So let's start out with a little bit of phthalo blue

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:24.280
and just tapping very little into my brush and just going to put sort of a circular pattern

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:30.000
of that up in here. Try to leave a couple light areas if you can. That helps add a little

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.820
to your sky and maybe a little bit of water at the same time.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:56.600
Okay, now I'm just going to start blending in my light area and trying to push that light

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:57.970
right out into the dark.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:09.120
And you just want your edges up at the top to sort of blend out into the rest of the painting.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:20.220
Now if you start getting any streaks or anything, you might need to wash your brush in between blending.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:38.880
to start with the fan brush and just load up a little bit of titanium white and we'll drop a

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:47.870
little cloud right in here just some little circular motions remember to try to keep the

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:54.260
top of the cloud nice and fluffy and the bottom of it should be sort of flat and maybe trying to

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:58.420
lead the eye into the center of the painting sort of coming down like this into the center

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:11.470
And don't be too particular on those clouds

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:14.680
because you want to make them just as quick as you can

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:17.680
or they come out just a tiny bit too perfect sometimes,

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:18.680
too symmetrical.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.800
Now I'm just going in with the big brush

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.670
and blending out the bottom of the cloud,

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.020
lifting up the top very gently.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:30.000
And you just go across it as light as possible.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:38.080
that kind of sets it down into the painting now with that same dirty fan

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:43.240
brush I can go into a little bit more titanium white and some more phthalo

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:51.580
blue and a little bit of midnight black and when you mix that together you end

00:03:51.580 --> 00:04:00.000
up with sort of a light blue color that'll be for our background pine trees and again try

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.790
I'm trying to lead the eye into the center of the painting.

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:06.150
So you're coming down in sort of a V pattern.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:08.570
I try to establish the pattern first

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:18.510
and then go back and make the trees more detailed.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:20.830
Notice how they get smaller towards the center.

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:24.230
That does help a lot.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.190
Then you can go in and make some little pointy tops

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:30.000
like that and just barely touch the brush with the corner.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:37.820
and make some pine tree indications.

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:39.320
Now remember, these are real far off,

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:43.850
so you don't wanna see too much detail.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:53.030
We'll work on that just a little later.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:58.290
It's kind of neat the way this painting came about,

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.000
'cause to be honest with you, I waited

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.340
about the last day before I was going to be on TV and was just sitting around with a buddy

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:11.120
of mine and decided, well, I'm going to have to come up with something here. And this is

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:18.730
what I came up with, kind of a top vignette idea, but the bottom stays full, which I thought

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:30.000
that was kind of different, kind of neat. Always trying to find new ideas. Speaking of new ideas,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.640
had a lot of people in our last seminar that we had out in New London, Connecticut do a

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:41.340
really good job. We're making all kinds of certified instructors out there. So there'll

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:48.310
be lots of people coming around teaching this technique. Okay, that would be enough on the

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:55.690
pine trees. Now let's go up with the two inch brush and do a little bit of mist. Always

00:05:55.690 --> 00:06:00.000
use the tip of the brush that way you get a nice variation of mist rather than one straight

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:09.030
and we can load the fan brush with a little bit more of that same color.

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:16.250
Maybe you'd want to add just a tiny bit more blue.

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:19.690
A lot of this is up to you, you know.

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:24.130
If I say you need to add something and you don't feel that you need to in your painting,

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:27.020
it's your world, do anything you want.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:30.000
This is kind of just to give you a guideline.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:34.620
go by. These are real easy hills, we're just tapping them in.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:39.990
Tap straight down with the brush and then you can just mist out the bottom of them.

00:06:39.990 --> 00:07:00.000
Get the lay of the land

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:07.050
kind of in a circular pattern around the bottom like this.

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:18.780
So I'm trying to follow that line, okay.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:21.100
Now I can go back and pull a reflection out of that

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:22.310
with a big brush.

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:32.790
Maybe I'd want to darken that just a little bit,

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:35.480
a little bit more phthalo blue.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:38.260
Reflections are so important in this painting

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:41.960
because it really doesn't have a whole lot in it

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:43.180
on this right-hand side,

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:46.770
so that reflection's going to be showing quite a bit.

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:57.320
Okay, let me just go over that.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:00.000
Okay.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.620
Drop a little shoreline in there.

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:06.080
Just using a little bit of liquid white on the knife.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:09.920
And kind of spread that around on your pallet a little bit.

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:16.810
And just cut across it.

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:18.950
Now you're just kind of sawing the line in

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:22.150
and make it come around the bend.

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:25.950
Drop lower on the left and right hand corners

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:26.880
than in the middle.

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:38.940
and try to keep 'em as straight as possible.

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:49.650
Okay, so you end up with something like this.

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:52.630
Now we can go in with a dark tree shape,

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:54.890
just using that same old dirty big brush,

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:57.820
except for we're gonna mix up a new color now.

00:08:57.820 --> 00:08:59.510
This'll be Sap Green.

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:05.600
Midnight Black, and just a touch of that Phthalo Blue.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:10.240
Quite a bit of Midnight Black in that.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:15.380
Okay, and then I'll take that dirty brush

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:16.820
that I just loaded that color on

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:19.760
and go over to a little bit of white.

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:23.840
And we end up with a very dull green.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:28.350
Okay, now I'm just gonna start tapping a dark shape in here.

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:29.800
I don't really know

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.330
exactly what it's supposed to look like being that it's going to be covered up later in the

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:42.190
painting so I just kind of tap in something. Keep a pine tree idea in mind with some fatter

00:09:42.190 --> 00:09:50.280
shapes for branches. The only thing that you'll see on this is these right-hand branches because

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:55.820
we're going to cover the rest of it up with a big tree in front so I just kind of throw it in.

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:20.640
Now with my liner brush, if I go through a little bit of this Van Dyke Brown, I can lay in a few branches.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:25.790
This has to be very thin though, I'd say at least five or six drops of paint thinner.

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:29.540
Spin that brush through the paint.

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:41.660
Now remember, don't get involved in a lot of detail here because this is all

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:49.080
gonna be in the background. And when you're using the liner brush sometimes the

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:51.060
quicker you do it the better it comes out.

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:57.120
That's something I think a lot of my students don't realize is sometimes the

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.880
harder you try on something the worse it comes out.

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:09.680
You have to sort of just relax and let it flow.

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:14.520
And don't try to make every branch, you know, real prominent.

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:17.820
Just let a few of them sort of stick out off the sides.

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:27.360
Some of them don't even have to be whole branches, just little hooks coming out like little starters.

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:38.520
Now, loading up a little bit more of that same color

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:41.160
under the big brush,

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:44.430
we're gonna try to set in the lay of the land here

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:46.760
down at the bottom.

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:48.240
There'll be some bushes coming out

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:50.320
and going around here like this,

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:52.400
and maybe there's another tree over here.

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:16.630
Okay, a few sticks.

00:12:16.630 --> 00:12:25.880
Just scratching them in with the tip of the knife.

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:28.830
Just start on our big poplar trees.

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:30.000
Some people call these birch trees.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:36.880
trees. Some people call them poplar trees. They can be either. Kind of depends on how big they get

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:46.360
on you. Okay, just loaded some Van Dyke Brown on my knife and just kind of throwing it right on

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:53.260
there. I just sort of get an idea of the shape and then go back and fill it in.

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:24.320
You want to make sure that it's getting progressively larger towards the bottom.

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:29.090
You don't want a big bump in there or anything that's going to make your tree look

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:44.190
strange. Let's see. We'll put another one right beside this one too. Maybe he has a little curve.

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:59.790
They say trees are like people and I believe that because every tree has a certain curve or bend in it.

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:05.950
Just like every person you know, have a curve and bend in them sometimes.

00:14:05.950 --> 00:14:07.740
Just depends.

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:12.200
This one right here, we'll put in just a little bit heavier

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:15.360
because we're going to need more white to pick up on the side of that

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:17.220
to get a brighter highlight.

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:24.130
And all of a sudden, he's a little bigger too.

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:29.960
That's a nice thing about this, if you make something a little bit too big,

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:39.440
no problem. It's really very tough to make a mistake in this style of painting.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:52.150
Okay, now wipe the knife off and get a little bit of titanium white on there. This is where you do

00:14:52.150 --> 00:15:00.000
that very, very light touch. Just bringing the white right across the front of the tree, mostly just on this right-hand

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:11.230
inside. And if you do it very light, kind of like the snow on the mountains, you'll

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:15.850
end up with a paper effect on the tree. It almost looks like you go up and peel it off

00:15:15.850 --> 00:15:24.460
after it dries. This sells paintings real well. People really like it when the paint

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:26.220
sticks out off the canvas like that.

00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:43.090
but you don't want to go back over those strokes very many times

00:15:43.090 --> 00:15:44.930
or you end up flattening it out too much.

00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:48.570
A little bit of phthalo blue and midnight black together

00:15:48.570 --> 00:15:53.130
for a shadow color and I just mix that real quick.

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:57.850
Leave it kind of marbled and with a roll of that you can come in

00:15:57.850 --> 00:16:00.000
on the other side and just barely any of it.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:17.970
that. Just a here and there type thing on that left hand side of the tree. Okay, now

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:25.090
we can start in with some heavier branches. This time I'll use Midnight Black for my branches

00:16:25.090 --> 00:16:29.760
instead of the brown. That way we'll get a darker, more close-up effect.

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:39.160
Now, if you're not having good luck on these branches, it might be because your paint's not thin enough.

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:46.060
But remember, most of everything we put in is going to be covered up later in the painting.

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:50.000
So you really don't have to spend a whole lot of time on this.

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:58.480
Sometimes the easiest way to do a painting is quick.

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:09.080
The first time I do one, I usually take about an hour on it, and then the second one, maybe a half hour.

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:20.980
People have asked me a lot of times, when you're on TV painting, don't you have to paint very fast, very quick?

00:17:21.340 --> 00:17:23.120
And the answer to that question is no.

00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:29.720
Sometimes I even have the problem of getting a painting done too quick if I'm not real careful.

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:37.380
so I have to stand here and flap my jaw a lot.

00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:41.000
Maybe just some small branches down here on the bottom.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:43.260
Keep in mind that each branch is gonna have

00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:44.640
some foliage on top of it,

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:48.250
so try to make 'em like a little arm sticking out there

00:17:48.250 --> 00:17:53.720
with a hand on the end of it holding up something.

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:57.460
I think about Ys and Vs when I'm doing these

00:17:57.460 --> 00:18:00.000
because you really don't wanna put three prongs

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:03.890
on one branch that are all sticking out together.

00:18:03.890 --> 00:18:08.570
It'll look like a pitchfork.

00:18:08.570 --> 00:18:11.870
If you have just one or two of those though,

00:18:11.870 --> 00:18:16.330
that wouldn't hurt anything.

00:18:16.330 --> 00:18:25.950
Okay, there's some darker branches.

00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:28.050
And now maybe I'll come back with that big brush

00:18:28.050 --> 00:18:29.830
in a really, really dark color.

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:35.700
Midnight Black and Sap Green with about 80% Midnight Black.

00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:40.160
And just getting a heal on the brush.

00:18:40.160 --> 00:18:43.280
And maybe we'll tap in a little like this.

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:46.160
Don't be scared of the big brush.

00:18:46.160 --> 00:18:48.670
It can be your best friend.

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:50.740
You just have to work with it and figure out

00:18:50.740 --> 00:18:54.130
how to manipulate it to do the right thing.

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:55.920
Just use small portions of the brush.

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:03.350
And don't be afraid to cover up your tree trunks a little bit.

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:07.480
In a lot of paintings that I see on the road,

00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:12.210
the tree will be totally exposed but yet there's leaves everywhere.

00:19:12.210 --> 00:19:16.470
And what that does is it makes your tree look very flat and undimensional.

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:20.640
So you need to put at least a couple leaves here and there

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:23.920
crossing over the actual bark of the tree.

00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:27.470
Helps push it back.

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:29.860
And maybe a little down at the bottom it sort of just

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:45.550
dyed there and at the same time you can darken up your bushes look pretty neat

00:19:45.550 --> 00:19:52.740
to have a little pond right there maybe I'll just pull a reflection and put a

00:19:52.740 --> 00:20:00.000
waterline under that and see what happens same thing as you did up above just offset

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:14.580
it a little bit. Okay, now how about a little bit of highlighting? Just dipping my brush

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:20.390
into the paint thinner, shaking off the excess, and then I'll go right through a little bit

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:30.000
of cadmium yellow and a little bit of bright red. See what we end up with. Kind of just

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:36.190
guess on a lot of my colors. I just figure out what season it's gonna be and

00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:39.350
then think about what I see in that season and try to get something that

00:20:39.350 --> 00:20:43.270
looks like it. I don't really think about the names of the colors or anything like

00:20:43.270 --> 00:21:00.000
that. The best way to do it is just by seeing it. Now I'm loading the brush very

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:06.210
very full of paint, but yet I'm not touching the canvas very hard. So those shadows remain

00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:14.080
underneath. I'm not destroying all my dark. And here we go across the tree trunk. See

00:21:14.080 --> 00:21:24.210
that really helps. I think a lot of the times the way I figure out new ideas in this style

00:21:24.210 --> 00:21:30.000
of painting is just by getting in one of those moods where, you know, maybe I don't

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:36.020
really feel like painting and making a mistake and then you learn something from that and

00:21:36.020 --> 00:21:40.890
when you get in a painting mood and come back and do it, then a lot of times it turns out

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:49.660
100% better. You just have to stick with anything to get it right. I'm going to leave some of

00:21:49.660 --> 00:21:58.310
my bushes back in there dark. Maybe I'll do a little color change. We'll go for a fireball right here.

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:08.610
And how about some green, we haven't done green yet.

00:22:08.610 --> 00:22:13.650
A little bit of phthalo blue with your cad yellow.

00:22:13.650 --> 00:22:18.050
Now, if your brush starts getting too dirty

00:22:18.050 --> 00:22:20.890
and you're picking up too much dark paint,

00:22:20.890 --> 00:22:23.100
your paint probably is not thin enough

00:22:23.100 --> 00:22:24.380
on your highlight color.

00:22:31.340 --> 00:22:32.840
So you gotta watch out for that.

00:22:32.840 --> 00:22:38.250
The consistency of the paint is of utmost importance here.

00:22:38.250 --> 00:22:42.930
Okay, now maybe we'll go into a little bit of liquid white

00:22:42.930 --> 00:22:54.850
with that same color and you get a nice light green.

00:22:54.850 --> 00:22:56.910
It's just possible that this tree over here

00:22:56.910 --> 00:23:00.000
might be showing some limbs too.

00:23:00.350 --> 00:23:02.250
on this right hand side.

00:23:02.250 --> 00:23:04.130
Maybe it comes right up out of the picture.

00:23:04.130 --> 00:23:26.150
Little bit of highlight on this one.

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:42.010
And down here.

00:23:42.010 --> 00:23:49.080
Maybe I'll just fill in some more of this,

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:55.830
that same dark color.

00:23:55.830 --> 00:23:59.960
This is my favorite part, just filling it all right in.

00:24:02.780 --> 00:24:06.080
Gives you a chance to get some of your frustrations

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:08.010
out from earlier in the painting.

00:24:08.010 --> 00:24:09.810
If you didn't like something you did.

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:11.470
You always want to see parts that you like,

00:24:11.470 --> 00:24:16.430
but at the same time there's going to be things you don't like.

00:24:16.430 --> 00:24:21.220
Let's take a little bit of Van Dyke Brown

00:24:21.220 --> 00:24:26.080
and just with a roll on the knife throw a path in.

00:24:26.080 --> 00:24:28.820
Real simple path idea.

00:24:28.820 --> 00:24:30.000
Just side to side.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:43.640
the way down, okay, and then you can come back with a little bit of dark sienna and

00:24:43.640 --> 00:24:49.180
van dyke brown and white, get a roll on the knife and go right over the top of that path.

00:24:49.180 --> 00:24:56.550
Just kind of caress over the thick pieces of brown and you'll end up with a highlight

00:24:56.550 --> 00:24:59.410
that breaks like rock formations and stuff like that.

00:25:01.370 --> 00:25:13.150
Okay, and then back into a little bit of our yellow and some more bushes, maybe another red one.

00:25:13.470 --> 00:25:15.880
I like red. I'm a red fan.

00:25:18.650 --> 00:25:22.110
A lot of the students in class ask me, how come you put red in your paintings like that?

00:25:22.150 --> 00:25:25.480
You don't see too much red in nature, but I tell them I just like it.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:31.900
And you can always go back to that,

00:25:31.900 --> 00:25:36.520
it's my world excuse.

00:25:36.520 --> 00:25:38.100
That's what I tell my dad.

00:25:38.100 --> 00:25:40.810
My dad walked in one day and he said,

00:25:40.810 --> 00:25:42.050
son, your room is a mess.

00:25:42.050 --> 00:25:43.730
It looks like a bomb hit in here.

00:25:43.730 --> 00:25:47.230
I said, it's my world.

00:25:47.230 --> 00:25:49.110
He didn't like that too much.

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:01.930
A little bit of yellow ochre and Indian yellow

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:13.810
as I start to get down here towards the left-hand corner.

00:26:13.810 --> 00:26:19.220
And there could be a little bit of dirt down here too.

00:26:19.220 --> 00:26:21.570
I like to keep my corners nice and dark.

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:42.360
Now wherever you see an area where it just sort of ends,

00:26:42.360 --> 00:26:47.380
to capture that effect even better, you can just mist it out a tiny bit.

00:26:47.380 --> 00:26:50.680
Okay, I think that's a completed painting.

00:26:50.680 --> 00:26:53.970
So I'll take my liner brush through a little bit of bright red

00:26:53.970 --> 00:26:56.440
and put a signature on this one.

00:27:05.050 --> 00:27:07.870
and we'll call that one done.

00:27:07.870 --> 00:27:09.250
Thanks for joining us today.

00:27:09.250 --> 00:27:10.610
I hope we can paint again sometime.

00:27:10.610 --> 00:27:11.450
Happy painting.
